.TH "HED-RELOAD" "1" "2026-08-11" "hed v1.19.0" "hed manual"
.SH NAME
hed\-reload \- Rebuild hed and replace the running process with the new
binary, without losing your open buffers.
.PP
Rebuild hed and replace the running process with the new binary, without
losing your open buffers.
.SH Usage
.IP
.EX
:reload
.EE
.PP
Steps:
.IP "1." 3
Run \f[CR]make \-j16\f[R] in the current working directory.
.IP "2." 3
If the build fails, abort and surface the status to you.
.IP "3." 3
If it succeeds, ask the \f[CR]session\f[R] plugin to write the
open\-buffer list to \f[CR]\[ti]/.cache/hed/<encoded\-cwd>/session\f[R].
.IP "4." 3
Set \f[CR]HED_RELOAD=1\f[R] in the environment.
.IP "5." 3
\f[CR]execl ./build/hed\f[R].
Same PID, fresh code.
.PP
After exec, the new process sees \f[CR]HED_RELOAD=1\f[R], reads the
session file via the \f[CR]session\f[R] plugin\[cq]s
\f[CR]HOOK_STARTUP_DONE\f[R] handler, reopens your buffers, and removes
the session file.
.SH Why a separate plugin
Reload is the only sensible name for a \[lq]rebuild and exec myself\[rq]
command, but the persistence half \[em] saving and restoring the open\-
buffer list \[em] is reusable.
So:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]reload\f[R] owns the build / exec mechanic.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]session\f[R] owns \f[CR]session_save\f[R] /
\f[CR]session_restore\f[R] and the \f[CR]HED_RELOAD\f[R] env var
contract.
.PP
If you load \f[CR]reload\f[R] without \f[CR]session\f[R],
\f[CR]:reload\f[R] still rebuilds and relaunches, but doesn\[cq]t reopen
buffers.
.SH Notes
.IP \[bu] 2
Assumes the cwd is the repo root and that \f[CR]./build/hed\f[R] is the
output.
If you run hed from elsewhere, \f[CR]:reload\f[R] won\[cq]t find the
binary to exec.
.IP \[bu] 2
The build step uses \f[CR]make \-j16\f[R].
Edit \f[CR]plugins/reload/reload.c\f[R] if you want a different
parallelism.
.IP \[bu] 2
Default leader binding: \f[CR]<space>rr\f[R] is wired to
\f[CR]:reload\f[R].
